<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!-- S1706: the watch splash reuses the phone's sampled launcher-icon blues so both surfaces
         show the same brand colour; the generator emits these names into the brand drawable.
         The watch composition is the logo alone - the wordmark and slogan colours the phone
         declares have no consumer here. -->
    <color name="splash_logo_gradient_start">#FF98CFF1</color>
    <color name="splash_logo_gradient_end">#FF0B82EB</color>
    <color name="tile_preview_bg">#FF202124</color>
    <color name="tile_preview_accent">#FF0B82EB</color>

    <!-- S2007: the calculator's running value carries its own colour so it reads as a result rather
         than as one more key label. S2003 may later fold it into a watch palette - it is one entry
         here, so that decision re-points it in one line. -->
    <color name="wear_calc_display_value">#FFFFD54F</color>

    <!-- S2152 ADR-2: the calculator menu's two tinted groups. Memory is warm, which is the family
         the owner named, but it is NOT the theme's error colour: S2007 ADR-4 gave that colour to a
         destructive action and stated no other control is drawn that way, so borrowing it would
         leave one tone carrying two meanings on one screen. The warm brown sits far enough from the
         translucent red plate the clear and backspace keys wear to stay a different statement.
         Functions take a cool slate so the two tinted groups read apart from each other and from the
         plain surface chip that history and close keep. Reversible: the owner asking for literally
         the same red is one value here. -->
    <color name="wear_calc_memory_tint">#FF5D4037</color>
    <color name="wear_calc_function_tint">#FF26343F</color>

    <!-- S2003 ADR-3: the semantic tone of a content type, mirrored from the phone's icon-tinting
         scheme so one entity wears one colour across both apps. Names match the phone's on purpose -
         the lineage stays greppable from either module when S2046 folds its four schemes into one.

         S2003 ADR-6: these are the night members of the phone's day/night pairs, and there is no
         values-night here. Wear Compose ships a single palette - androidx.wear.compose.material
         .Colors declares no isLight and defaults background to black - so the watch has one surface
         and a split would resolve a choice that does not exist, at the risk of handing the watch the
         dark member of each pair against a black background. -->
    <color name="color_media_music">#FF4B7A74</color>
    <color name="color_media_video">#FF80608C</color>
    <color name="color_media_image">#FF51789A</color>
    <color name="color_media_docs">#FF9A7445</color>
    <color name="color_media_other">#FF77848A</color>

    <!-- S2003 ADR-4: the only two tones in this set with no phone-side source - neither folder nor
         stream is a content type in any of the phone's four schemes. They are defined here, not
         inherited, and are marked so a later consolidation reads them as original rather than as
         drift. Green and red were chosen for being unoccupied and furthest from the five above;
         grey and teal were rejected because "other" and music already hold them. -->
    <color name="color_media_folder">#FF628467</color>
    <color name="color_media_stream">#FF9A5A5A</color>
</resources>
